Z||000000||Buster||07-05-2001||05:41 PM||||Rear 88 caliper. I was no where close to the torgue spec, ( if the Haynes is correct ) and it stripped the aluminum casting. Has anyone used a Helicoil or other fix. This was rebuilt so maybe it was already stretched.  Thanks   Buster||||reg||1
Z||000001||William Federle||07-05-2001||07:44 PM||||I have a 1984 Fiero and I replaced the rear calipers. I put the old banjo bolts back in and the left one stripped. When I got it out it had the aluminum threads still in its threads. The guy at Autozone gave me another caliper and just to be sure, I bought a new banjo bolt. I was able to get the specified torque on it and it didn't strip. I think that whenever I take a banjo fitting off of a caliper I will always use a new banjo bolt. <P>A new bolt might tighten up in your caliper.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||07-05-2001||11:01 PM||||Get another rebuilt.<P>The AL calipers are often damaged by previous persons taking out the bolt. (Not always because they are stupid... it just the way of them.)<P>Don't heliciol these. The copper won't likely cover the coil and the AL well enough to gurantee a lasting seal.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fiero_Freak||07-05-2001||11:04 PM||||Yeah..Don't use helicoils in these.  I think I went through about 8 calipers when I didn't even need to take the damn things off in the first place.  Almost every reman was helicoiled and they all pulled out.  God that was pissing me off...||||reg||1
